    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Charif Souki is an American energy entrepreneur who built substantial wealth through Cheniere Energy and later co-founded Tellurian. Educated at Colgate and Columbia, he worked in investment banking before developing liquefied natural gas businesses. He chairs Ajax Holdings, his family's private investment company, whose activities include property, hospitality and energy.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Charif Souki's gas import terminal at Sabine Pass faced obsolescence almost as soon as it opened. The shale boom had made American gas abundant, undermining the shortage on which the project depended. Souki responded by pursuing an export plant, adding equipment to cool domestic gas into liquid for overseas shipment.\n\nFinancing depended on customers as well as construction. Earlier agreements with Chevron and Total provided payments that helped the business survive when imports failed to arrive. Souki then secured long-term export contracts and attracted institutional capital for the conversion. The project required federal approvals alongside engineering work, making commercial negotiation and regulatory persistence central to his achievement.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Full contemporary feature establishes the reversal, contracted cash flow, new financing and approvals. Later reporting confirms the eventual conversion; no prediction presented as completed history."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 107,
    "html": "<p>Charif Souki's gas import terminal at Sabine Pass faced obsolescence almost as soon as it opened. The shale boom had made American gas abundant, undermining the shortage on which the project depended. Souki responded by pursuing an export plant, adding equipment to cool domestic gas into liquid for overseas shipment.</p>\r\n<p>Financing depended on customers as well as construction. Earlier agreements with Chevron and Total provided payments that helped the business survive when imports failed to arrive. Souki then secured long-term export contracts and attracted institutional capital for the conversion. The project required federal approvals alongside engineering work, making commercial negotiation and regulatory persistence central to his achievement.</p>\r\n"
  },
